To add Swagger (using swagger-jsdoc and swagger-ui-express) for API documentation in the Node.js app, I'll guide you through an update of the previous example. Swagger will provide a user-friendly interface for API documentation.

Step 1: Install Swagger Dependencies
Run the following command to install Swagger-related dependencies:

bash
Copy code
npm install swagger-jsdoc swagger-ui-express
Step 2: Update the Express App with Swagger
We'll update app.js to include Swagger documentation setup.

Here's the updated app.js:

js
Copy code
// app.js
const express = require('express');
const swaggerJsdoc = require('swagger-jsdoc');
const swaggerUi = require('swagger-ui-express');

const app = express();

// Swagger configuration
const swaggerOptions = {
    definition: {
        openapi: '3.0.0',
        info: {
            title: 'Node Test API',
            version: '1.0.0',
            description: 'A simple Express API with Swagger documentation',
        },
    },
    apis: ['./app.js'], // Points to the API documentation in the source code
};

// Initialize Swagger docs
const swaggerDocs = swaggerJsdoc(swaggerOptions);
app.use('/api-docs', swaggerUi.serve, swaggerUi.setup(swaggerDocs));

/**
 * @swagger
 * /:
 *   get:
 *     summary: Returns a Hello, World! message
 *     responses:
 *       200:
 *         description: Success
 *         content:
 *           application/json:
 *             schema:
 *               type: object
 *               properties:
 *                 message:
 *                   type: string
 *                   example: Hello, World!
 */
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
    res.status(200).json({ message: 'Hello, World!' });
});

module.exports = app;
Step 3: Start the Server
No changes are needed in server.js. You can start the server with:

bash
Copy code
node server.js
Step 4: Access the Swagger Documentation
After starting the server, you can access the Swagger UI in your browser at:

bash
Copy code
http://localhost:3000/api-docs
This will show a web-based interface for your API documentation. The GET / route should appear with a description.

Step 5: Updating Tests (Optional)
If you want to add a test to verify that Swagger is set up correctly, you can update tests/app.test.js:

js
Copy code
// tests/app.test.js
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../app');

describe('GET /', () => {
    it('should return a 200 status and a Hello, World! message', async () => {
        const response = await request(app).get('/');
        expect(response.statusCode).toBe(200);
        expect(response.body).toHaveProperty('message', 'Hello, World!');
    });
});

describe('GET /api-docs', () => {
    it('should return a 200 status for the Swagger UI', async () => {
        const response = await request(app).get('/api-docs');
        expect(response.statusCode).toBe(200);
    });
});
Now your tests include a check to make sure the Swagger UI endpoint is accessible.

Explanation
swagger-jsdoc is used to generate the OpenAPI (Swagger) documentation based on JSDoc comments in the code.
swagger-ui-express serves the Swagger UI at the /api-docs endpoint.
The JSDoc comments (@swagger) are used to document each route. In the example, the GET / route has a description and response example.
Additional Swagger Configuration
You can add more detailed documentation by extending the JSDoc comments for other routes. Here's an example of a more detailed route:

js
Copy code
/**
 * @swagger
 * /hello:
 *   get:
 *     summary: Returns a greeting message
 *     responses:
 *       200:
 *         description: Success
 *         content:
 *           application/json:
 *             schema:
 *               type: object
 *               properties:
 *                 message:
 *                   type: string
 *                   example: Hello, API User!
 */
app.get('/hello', (req, res) => {
    res.status(200).json({ message: 'Hello, API User!' });
});
This will automatically appear in the Swagger UI when you access /api-docs.